this fuel crisis is a consequence of  demand not supply there's plenty of  diesel and petrol it's just not always  in the right place because there aren't  enough drivers to deliver it the problem  is us buying more than the industry was  prepared for now this is what the  national fuel stock usually looks like  it's the average percentage of storage  being used at garages every week since  the start of last year the top line is  diesel the bottom one petrol in the  middle line is the average you can see  it fluctuates it went up storage during  lockdowns when we were at home and then  it went down when restrictions were  eased and we hit the road but the really  interesting thing is the average is  around 40 percent of storage at any time  so those massive tanks under garages are  never full because 40 is more than  enough to manage normal demand in that  sense the national stock is a bit like  your personal stock it's more economical  and efficient to run your car half full  as long as you're confident you can get  more fuel when you need it now this is  what normal sales look like over the  same period the blue line is the average  weekly sales at a sample of the uk's  filling stations the green line the  pre-covered average and again sales fell  sharply during lockdown one but by last  month they were nudging back to 17 000  liters a week that's 92  of pre-lock down levels of course last  week sales went through the roof the  petrol retailers association says as a  consequence while deliveries are now  getting through 37 of its forecourts  that's up to 2 000 sites still have no  fuel the problems began last friday when  hsbc says it processed 50 more payments  at petrol stations than the week before  with the average spend increasing from  20 pounds to 30 pounds and halfords says  it sold 17 times more jerry cans than  normal at the weekend now the national  emergency plan for fuel lists 10 steps  ministers can take in a crisis five to  maintain fuel supply three of which  they've already activated including  drafting in army drivers and five more  to manage supply and demand including  rationing sales and prioritizing access  for key workers the prime minister says  supplies are stabilizing but if any of  these levers are pulled we'll know that  consumers are skeptical when ministers  say we should calm down perhaps after 18  months of immense disruption we're not  ready to take the chance they might be  wrong
 

